Topi Linkala wrote:
> Derek Ray wrote:
>> Topi Linkala wrote:
>>> There clearly is a constraint on how many items can exist in a single
>>> moment in the game, but the idea that after some items are destroyed
>>> more can be _always_ generated, I would assume, is deliberate.
>>
>> Ah, but what if your assumptions are wrong?
> 
> That extinction doesn't affect quest monster creation, is deliberate,

That seems likely to be deliberate, yes.

> creating new items is deliberate as death drops are deliberate.

Deathdrops' creating items is obviously intentional.  But limitless
items?  Ah, now we're in speculative territory.

> My assumptions are that quest monsters don't follow extinction rules, so
> that one cannot make the quest a cakewalk 

Or (more likely, IMO) prevent making the post-quest game more easy by
simply doing the Quest and hanging out 'til you blow away 120 monsters
of a particular type, and then knowing that no more will be generated.
The dragons on the Healer quest come to mind... if you extinct dragons
before the Castle, are there guards on the storerooms?

It may not even have been related to making anything less "easy" -- just
a knowledge that when you restrict monster generation to only a few
types, a player could easily extinct those types before reaching the
bottom of the Quest, and it would seem silly to provide a built-in
method of near-guaranteed extinction of a few monster types each game.

> and that death drop is there
> exactly for the reason to introduce random items to the game such a way
> that the player can have the first opportunity to use them.

There's a difference here, though.  Death drops are there to introduce
random items into the game.  But do you think the Quest was intended to
be a limitless source of items?

Then again, there's also the fact that you have to spend a great deal of
time sitting and waiting for monsters to spawn if you're going to try to
farm Quest creatures; perhaps the Dev Team looked at it and said
"Possible, but not worth fixing because it's such a hideous amount of
manual effort to do".

There's some noticeable difference between this and scripted pudding
farming, for sure.  I'm inclined to say that it should be fixed as well
just on general principles, but the above case I mentioned gives me some
pause since one really _can't_ have the Quest monsters default to
automatic extinction for the type... rendering any fix slightly more
complicated, though far from impossible.  (perhaps any of that monster
type generated after 120 simply no longer provides XP or deathdrops, as
has been suggested in numerous other threads.)

